.\" The manual page of taco(1). Source: the usage text in crates/taco-cli/src/args.rs and the
.\" planned model commands of docs/design/model-distribution.md. Keep the two in agreement.
.\" Sections marked PLANNED describe commands and options that this version does not have.
.\" Check the page with: mandoc -T lint docs/man/taco.1
.TH TACO 1 2026-10-19 "taco 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
taco \- lossless compression with an integer language model
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B taco fold
.I input
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RB [ \-\-model
.IR path.hsm ]
.RB [ \-\-profile
.BR text | logs | auto ]
.RB [ \-\-block\-size
.IR bytes ]
.RB [ \-\-backend
.BR auto | cuda | gpu | cpu ]
.RB [ \-\-threads
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.PP
.B taco unfold
.I input.taco
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RB [ \-\-model
.IR path.hsm ]
.RB [ \-\-backend
.BR auto | cuda | gpu | cpu ]
.RB [ \-\-threads
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.PP
.B taco
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.PP
PLANNED:
.B taco model
.BR list | fetch | verify | import | path
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
PLANNED:
.B taco
.BR \-V | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B taco
is a lossless compressor.
A language model (GPT\-2 small) predicts the next token of the data, and a range coder keeps
only the part that the model did not predict.
The engine, HardShell, uses only integer arithmetic.
Thus every machine calculates the same predictions, bit for bit, and a file that one machine
folds unfolds on every other machine.
.PP
.B taco fold
compresses
.I input
into
.IR input .taco.
.B taco unfold
restores
.I input.taco
into the input name without
.BR .taco .
The container stores the SHA\-256 of the original data.
.B taco unfold
verifies it, and a mismatch is an error, never a wrong output.
.PP
The container also stores the SHA\-256 of the model file.
.B taco unfold
refuses a model file with a different hash.
A block that does not become smaller is stored raw, so the output grows by the header and a
small amount for each block only.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B fold
Compress
.I input
into
.IR input .taco,
or into the file of
.BR \-o .
.TP
.B unfold
Restore
.I input.taco
into the input name without
.BR .taco ,
or into the file of
.BR \-o .
The input name must end with
.B .taco
when
.B \-o
is not given.
.TP
.B help
Show the usage text.
The same as
.BR \-\-help .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIpath\fR"
The output file.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-model " \fIpath.hsm\fR"
The model file.
The default is the file in the environment variable
.BR TACO_MODEL .
.B fold
needs a model.
.B unfold
needs the model whose hash is in the container, except for a container that holds only raw
blocks.
PLANNED: the value can also be a model name (for example
.BR gpt2 )
or a hash, and
.B taco
then finds the file in the model directories (see MODEL FILES).
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " name"
.B fold
only.
.B text
uses GPT\-2 alone.
.B logs
uses GPT\-2, the match model and the mixer.
.B auto
(the default) uses
.B logs
for a block that gets smaller with it, else
.BR text .
.B unfold
reads the profile from the file.
.TP
.BI \-\-block\-size " bytes"
.B fold
only.
The number of input bytes in each block.
The default is 16384.
The minimum is 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-backend " name"
Where the model runs.
.B cuda
uses an NVIDIA GPU through the CUDA driver.
.B gpu
uses wgpu (Metal, Vulkan, DX12 or OpenGL).
.B cpu
uses the processor.
.B auto
(the default) uses the first of
.BR cuda ,
.B gpu
and
.B cpu
that passes a self\-test.
The output is the same on every backend.
.B fold
with the profile
.B logs
or
.B auto
runs on the CPU.
An explicit
.B cuda
or
.B gpu
that does not work is an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-threads " n"
The number of worker threads.
The default is all CPUs.
The output does not change with the number of threads.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Replace an output file that exists.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the usage text.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
PLANNED.
Show the version of
.BR taco ,
the container versions that it reads and writes, and the hashes of the models that it knows.
.TP
.B \-\-offline
PLANNED.
.BR fold ,
.B unfold
and
.B model fetch
do not download a model.
The same as
.BR TACO_OFFLINE=1 .
.SH MODEL COMMANDS (PLANNED)
These commands are not in this version.
.I docs/design/model\-distribution.md
specifies them.
A model is named by its name (for example
.BR gpt2 )
or by its SHA\-256 (64 hexadecimal digits, or a unique prefix of 8 or more digits).
.TP
.BR "taco model list" " [\-\-all]"
Show each model that
.B taco
knows, its SHA\-256, its size, and the path of the file if the file is in a model directory.
With
.BR \-\-all ,
also show the other model files in the model directories.
.TP
.BR "taco model fetch" " [\fIname\fR|\fIhash\fR ...] [\-\-all] [\-\-offline]"
Download each named model (the default is the models of the profiles
.B text
and
.BR logs ),
verify its SHA\-256, and put it in the user model directory.
A model that is already there is not downloaded again.
.TP
.BR "taco model verify" " [\fIname\fR|\fIhash\fR|\fIpath\fR ...]"
Calculate the SHA\-256 of each model file and compare it with the published hash.
With no argument, verify every file in the model directories.
.TP
.BR "taco model import" " \fIpath\fR [\-\-expect \fIhash\fR]"
Verify the file at
.I path
and copy it into the user model directory.
Use this command on a machine without network access.
.TP
.BR "taco model path" " \fIname\fR|\fIhash\fR"
Show the path of the model file.
The exit status is 1 if no model directory has the file.
.SH MODEL FILES
A model file
.RB ( .hsm )
holds the integer weights, the tables and the vocabulary.
A model file never changes after its release.
Its SHA\-256 identifies it.
.PP
This version uses the file of
.B \-\-model
or
.BR TACO_MODEL .
PLANNED: when neither is given,
.B taco
looks for the file
.IB hash .hsm
in these directories, in this sequence, and uses the first file that it finds:
.IP 1. 4
The directories in
.B TACO_MODEL_PATH
(separated by
.B :
or, on Windows,
.BR ; ).
.PD 0
.IP 2. 4
The user model directory:
.B TACO_MODEL_DIR
if it is set, else
.I ~/.local/share/taco/models
on Linux
.RB ( $XDG_DATA_HOME/taco/models
if
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is set),
.I ~/Library/Application Support/taco/models
on macOS, and
.I %LOCALAPPDATA%\etaco\emodels
on Windows.
.IP 3. 4
The directory
.I models
next to the
.B taco
program.
.IP 4. 4
The system model directory:
.I /usr/local/share/taco/models
and
.I /usr/share/taco/models
on Linux,
.I /Library/Application Support/taco/models
on macOS, and
.I %PROGRAMDATA%\etaco\emodels
on Windows.
.PD
.PP
PLANNED: if no directory has the file,
.B taco
downloads it one time into the user model directory and verifies its SHA\-256 before use.
.B \-\-offline
or
.B TACO_OFFLINE=1
prevents the download.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TACO_MODEL
The model file, if
.B \-\-model
is not given.
.TP
.B TACO_MODEL_DIR
PLANNED.
The user model directory.
.TP
.B TACO_MODEL_PATH
PLANNED.
More directories to search for model files, before the user model directory.
.TP
.B TACO_OFFLINE
PLANNED.
If the value is
.BR 1 ,
.B taco
does not download a model.
.TP
.B TACO_MODEL_URL
PLANNED.
A base URL that replaces the built\-in download locations, for example a mirror on a private
network.
.B taco
downloads
.IB TACO_MODEL_URL / hash .hsm
and verifies its SHA\-256.
.TP
.B WGPU_BACKEND
Limits the wgpu backend to one of
.BR vulkan ,
.BR metal ,
.B dx12
or
.BR gl .
.TP
.B WGPU_DX12_COMPILER
On Windows, the DX12 shader compiler of wgpu:
.B dxc
needs
.I dxcompiler.dll
on the search path.
See NOTES.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error: a file that cannot be read or written, a model with the wrong hash, a damaged
container, a checksum mismatch, or a backend that does not work.
.TP
.B 2
The arguments are not valid.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB name .taco
A container: the header, the block table, the blocks, the model hash and the checksum.
.TP
.IB hash .hsm
A model file in a model directory (PLANNED).
.SH EXAMPLES
Compress a file, then restore it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
taco fold notes.txt \-\-model gpt2.hsm
taco unfold notes.txt.taco \-\-model gpt2.hsm
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compress a log file with the profile logs into a given output, on the CPU:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
export TACO_MODEL=$HOME/models/gpt2.hsm
taco fold app.log \-\-profile logs \-\-backend cpu \-o app.log.taco
.EE
.RE
.PP
PLANNED: download the models one time, then work without a network:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
taco model fetch
taco model verify
TACO_OFFLINE=1 taco unfold notes.txt.taco
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The speed depends on the backend.
.B fold
is faster than
.BR unfold ,
because
.B unfold
decodes one token after the other in each block.
.PP
The CUDA backend needs an NVIDIA driver and a GPU with compute capability 8.0 or more.
It needs no CUDA toolkit: the kernels are in the program, and the driver compiles them at run
time.
.PP
On Windows, the DX12 backend of wgpu needs the DXC shader compiler
.RI ( dxcompiler.dll ).
Without it, wgpu uses the old FXC compiler, which cannot compile all kernels of
.BR taco .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xz (1),
.BR zstd (1)
.PP
